<engine:UXML xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:engine="UnityEngine.UIElements" xmlns:editor="UnityEditor.UIElements" editor-extension-mode="False">
    <!--
        Comfort view anchor: one small static dot at the centre of vision. OFF by default.

        ── What this is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        It is not an aiming reticle, and calling it one would make it actively misleading. This game
        aims with the CONTROLLER: the creative input bridge raycasts from the XRI ray interactor's
        origin, and the block a trigger press will break is wherever the hand points — almost never
        the centre of the view. Target feedback already exists in the right place, in world space,
        as PlacementPreview.

        The FPV HUD report treats a centre reticle as a HUD element to migrate. It is not one: this
        repository has never had a reticle, and the report's own anchoring table asks for something
        this project's aiming model cannot support.

        ── What it IS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        A comfort feature. Seok et al. found a first-person visual guide correlated with reduced
        VR-sickness measures, and the mechanism has nothing to do with aiming: a fixed visual
        reference gives the vestibular system something stationary to hold onto while the world
        slides past during continuous locomotion. That benefit stands whether or not the dot marks
        anything. The same work warns that a LARGER guide costs presence, which is why this is a dot
        rather than a cross, and why growing it "to be safe" is the wrong instinct.

        It therefore lives with the comfort options next to the vignette, not with the HUD settings,
        and it is off until the player asks for it.

        ── Placement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        Dead centre with NO pitch, unlike every other HUD-family panel. The rest of the family is
        tilted 12° down because it sits below the eyeline and should face the player; this one sits
        ON the view axis, where a tilt would push it off centre.

        Read-only: every element is picking-mode Ignore, so the panel generates no collider. A
        collider at the exact centre of the player's view would intercept every world interaction
        they attempt — the single worst place in the whole HUD to put one.
    -->
    <engine:VisualElement name="bv-screen-root" class="hs-screen va-root" picking-mode="Ignore">
        <engine:VisualElement name="bv-view-anchor" class="va-dot" picking-mode="Ignore" />
    </engine:VisualElement>
</engine:UXML>
